+## 7. Ramification class and degrees
+
+The normal-singular incidence integration identifies the resolved source
+different with the Cartier pullback `r^*R_X~2A+B`, connected and containing
+every exceptional curve (`NS:205-263`).  In the `F_2` marking,
+
+```text
+r^*R_X=2A+B=4S_0+11F-2sum_i E_i.
+```
+
+Separate strict nonexceptional ramification into noncontracted divisorial
+primes `C_j`, contracted affine primes `Z_k`, and the actual ADE exceptional
+cycle `M=sum m_alpha E_alpha`.  The fixed-sheet theorem applies only over a
+target divisor: in degree three a ramified divisorial branch has an
+unramified sheet plus one tame transposition, so its different coefficient is
+one (`GB:47-64`).  It does not control a curve mapped to a target point.
+
+The binding equations are therefore
+
+```text
+sum_j C_j+sum_k nu_k Z_k+M
+    =4S_0+11F-2sum_i E_i,
+
+sum_j A.C_j=8,
+sum_j B.C_j+sum_k nu_k=4,
+nu_k>=1.
+```
+
+In particular `nu_k<=4`, but multiplicity one is not proved for contracted
+primes.  At each ADE tree `n=Cm>=0`, with `m>0`, and `M` must be computed in
+the actual root classes, not by assigning the same coefficients to the
+orthogonal total-transform symbols (`P:473-527`, `AD:70-169`).
+
+For a smooth F5 point, the local different has two reduced smooth branches
+with exact boundary totals five and three (`LD:77-158`).  They cannot be one
+global prime without making a rational-forest cycle (`LD:160-190`), so the
+global decomposition has distinct `A`-degrees `3` and `5`.  For singular
+`p_0`, no analogous split is licensed; only a positive partition of eight
+among noncontracted strict primes is known.
+
+Distinct noncontracted `C_j` must have distinct target divisorial images and
+map generically birationally to them; otherwise the degree-three fibre
+accounting with the fixed sheet is exceeded.  This should remain explicit in
+the promoted state.
